{
  "requirement": "Preserves the selector and a diagnostic explaining the syntax error.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct SelectorError {\n    pub selector: String,\n    pub message: String,\n}\n\nimpl SelectorError {\n    pub fn new(selector: &str, message: impl Into<String>) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for SelectorError;\nimpl Debug for SelectorError;\nimpl Display for SelectorError;\nimpl Error for SelectorError;\nimpl PartialEq for SelectorError;\nimpl Eq for SelectorError;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover `requirements.sdk.outlines.SelectorError`.\n\n**Obligations derived from the requirement**\n- The error preserves the selector and a diagnostic message.\n- `SelectorError::new(&str, impl Into<String>)` constructs it.\n- It implements `Clone`, `Debug`, `Display`, `Error`, `PartialEq` and `Eq`.\n- It is `#[non_exhaustive]` with public `selector` and `message` fields.\n\n**Test coverage**\n`crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]` feeds seven invalid selectors to `parse_selector`: `fn`, `fn[run`, `[run]`, `fn[run]#0`, `use@123`, `fn[run]x` and `a//b`. For each one it checks the following:\n- **Selector preserved:** `error.selector` equals the input selector.\n- **Message present:** `error.message` is non-empty.\n- **`new`, `Clone`, `PartialEq`, `Debug`:** `assert_eq!(error.clone(), SelectorError::new(invalid, &error.message))`.\n  - It shows `new` stores both fields. Swapped or dropped fields would fail.\n  - It also exercises `Clone` and `PartialEq`, and `assert_eq!` requires `Debug`.\n  - `new` accepts `&String` through `Into<String>`.\n- **`Display` and `Error`:** the error is used as `&dyn std::error::Error`. Its `to_string()` must contain both the selector and the message.\n\n**Items with no dedicated test**\n- I read the implementation only to check for conditionals. `Clone`, `Debug`, `PartialEq` and `Eq` are plain `#[derive]`s with no logic of their own. `Eq` is a compile-time marker. The `Error` impl is an empty body.\n- `#[non_exhaustive]` cannot be tested from inside the defining crate. The public API inventory test in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs` covers the export itself.\n\nI found no gap and no acknowledgments apply.",
